An abstract invariant joins possible stores at a location; a symbolic state usually describes stores reached along a selected prefix. The two descriptions can be combined only after they are brought into a common coordinate system. This chapter develops the bridge from symbolic expressions over inputs to constraints over current stores. The comparison draws on \emph{Abstract Interpretation, Symbolic Execution and Constraints} by Amadini and colleagues and on Patrick and Radhia Cousot's account of concretization and sound approximation in \emph{Abstract Interpretation Frameworks}.

\section{A Relation Between Inputs and Current Stores}

Let $u$ range over input valuations, let $s$ range over current stores, and write $\sigma(u)$ for the evaluation of all symbolic terms under $u$. On an exact prefix $p$, the symbolic state describes
\[
 R_p=\{(u,s)\mid \phi_p(u)\land s=\sigma_p(u)\}.
\]
Projection onto $s$ forgets how those stores were obtained. An abstract location annotation $a_\ell$ over stores satisfies $\mathrm{Reach}_\ell\subseteq\gamma(a_\ell)$, so for a reached prefix at $\ell$ we also have $\{s\mid (u,s)\in R_p\text{ for some }u\}\subseteq\gamma(a_\ell)$. If $A_\ell$ expresses the concretization, conjoining $A_\ell(\sigma_p(u))$ to the path condition cannot remove a real execution. This explains why inserting an invariant does not add information to the semantics of an already exact path. It can still expose a cheap contradiction to an analyzer or to a solver that would otherwise have to derive the invariant from a much larger expression.

Do not match constraints to variable names alone. A constraint over the value of $x$ after an assignment does not necessarily constrain the input that originally occupied $x$. Use versioned variables or substitute the current terms; rename auxiliaries at different locations and include the applicable call context. For a loop summary or a branch with approximate external effects, conjoining a sound invariant \emph{can} eliminate impossible states introduced by the approximation, without making the result exact in general.

\section{A Relational Test That Intervals Cannot Discharge}

A widget layout routine receives an integer $N\in[0,9]$ and sets its left and right positions to $x:=N$ and $y:=N+2$. One postcondition is $x<y$. Plain intervals give $x\in[0,9]$ and $y\in[2,11]$; the store $(9,2)$ satisfies those bounds and violates the contract. It is not a test case. With an abstract difference constraint $y-x=2$, checking $y\le x$ in the abstract state is already contradictory. In an exact symbolic state the same check is $N+2\le N$ with $0\le N\le9$, also contradictory. These answers agree even though the abstract relation alone does not identify the input behind each store.

Now alter the routine to set $y:=N$ on an optional compact-layout branch. A join cannot retain $y-x=2$ as an invariant; it can retain $0\le y-x\le2$. This does not refute $y\le x$ at the join. The exact compact prefix does produce a model, for example $N=0$ with compact layout enabled. A concrete replay gives $(0,0)$ and establishes the failure of the strict-order contract. Strengthening the join by reusing the old invariant would suppress a real test. A regression for the combined engine should check both revisions, verifying inductiveness before allowing any annotation to prune paths.

\section{Information Can Flow in Both Directions}

Alatawi, S{\o}ndergaard, and Miller illustrate how to preserve initial values as extra variables so that relational invariants describe indirect effects of input-dependent looping. An engine can use such an invariant to constrain a summary with fresh values for exit variables. A satisfiable model of that summary is a \emph{candidate} input because the summary may describe impossible executions. Replaying it is part of producing a witness; if replay disagrees, one can refine the summary or explore a more precise prefix. Conversely, checked symbolic infeasibility queries can reveal predicates worth adding to the abstract domain, but merely observing the same fact on many feasible tests does not establish an invariant. Check the refined annotation at entry and on every modeled incoming edge. Check its scope as well: a fact proved assuming a function argument is nonnegative can prune exploration in a caller only if that caller satisfies the premise. Combining the methods is most useful when the implementation distinguishes what has been proved unreachable, what has been observed on a concrete run, and what is still possible only according to an approximation.
